Oh, there seems to be an error.

00:12:10.070 --> 00:12:13.360
So it says that the remote
access server is not enabled,

00:12:13.360 --> 00:12:14.700
or the machine's not running.

00:12:14.700 --> 00:12:19.900
So I can definitely see
that the machine's running.

00:12:19.900 --> 00:12:23.170
I saw that RTP was
configured on my instance.

00:12:23.170 --> 00:12:24.400
So what do I do now?

00:12:24.400 --> 00:12:26.710
So we have interactive--
we have ISPAC.

00:12:26.710 --> 00:12:31.100
So I'm going to connect to the
serial port really quickly.

00:12:31.100 --> 00:12:33.340
And just to save some time
while this [INAUDIBLE],

00:12:33.340 --> 00:12:36.740
then I'll switch back, I
actually already logged in.

00:12:36.740 --> 00:12:38.304
So let's see if it reconnects.

00:12:46.702 --> 00:12:50.910
All right, I'm going to
punch in my password.

00:12:50.910 --> 00:12:51.744
And there you go.

00:12:51.744 --> 00:12:53.160
If you are a Windows
SS admin, you

00:12:53.160 --> 00:12:54.840
will recognize this console.

00:12:54.840 --> 00:12:57.070
You didn't have to
leave the web browser.

00:12:57.070 --> 00:12:58.620
And you have access to this.

00:12:58.620 --> 00:13:00.590
Now, I can run
things, like, hey,

00:13:00.590 --> 00:13:02.310
did it also lose
internet connectivity?

00:13:02.310 --> 00:13:03.791
Is this actually connected?

00:13:03.791 --> 00:13:04.665
So I'm going to see--

00:13:07.320 --> 00:13:10.350
yeah, it seems to be connected
to the internet fine.

00:13:10.350 --> 00:13:13.200
Most of the problems that
we see with RTP not working

00:13:13.200 --> 00:13:14.744
is the Windows Firewall.

00:13:14.744 --> 00:13:16.660
The rules are not enabled
or it's just not on.

00:13:16.660 --> 00:13:22.595
So let's just do a quick check
on what the Windows Firewall

00:13:22.595 --> 00:13:23.095
status is.

00:13:27.400 --> 00:13:29.560
Oh, the Windows Firewall is off.

00:13:29.560 --> 00:13:31.730
No wonder RTP is not working.

00:13:31.730 --> 00:13:35.560
So let's go and restart that.

00:13:42.220 --> 00:13:43.230
I think I made a--

00:13:46.420 --> 00:13:48.050
let's type that again.

00:13:48.050 --> 00:13:49.360
Apparently I can't type today.

00:13:57.290 --> 00:13:57.930
There you go.

00:13:57.930 --> 00:13:59.510
So the Windows
Firewall has started.

00:13:59.510 --> 00:14:01.680
Now, let me see if that
really fixed that issue.

00:14:01.680 --> 00:14:03.530
So let me go up.

00:14:03.530 --> 00:14:06.560
And really from the
browser, try to RTP in--

00:14:06.560 --> 00:14:09.920
super convenient.

00:14:09.920 --> 00:14:10.970
There you go.

00:14:10.970 --> 00:14:13.280
Someone messed up
the Windows Firewall.

00:14:13.280 --> 00:14:14.570
That was me.

00:14:14.570 --> 00:14:16.790
But this was just
for demo purposes.

00:14:16.790 --> 00:14:19.190
So switching back to slides.

00:14:19.190 --> 00:14:21.110
So you can see that
we really worked hard

00:14:21.110 --> 00:14:23.240
on making sure that sudden
difficult problems are

00:14:23.240 --> 00:14:24.872
made really super easy for you.

00:14:24.872 --> 00:14:26.330
And that's the
advantage of running

00:14:26.330 --> 00:14:29.120
Windows Workloads on us.

00:14:29.120 --> 00:14:30.270
So we had that quick demo.

00:14:30.270 --> 00:14:31.854
So now you've deployed
these machines,

00:14:31.854 --> 00:14:34.103
but you're not going to do
this one machine at a time.

00:14:34.103 --> 00:14:36.200
You want to deploy this
in a scalable fashion.

00:14:36.200 --> 00:14:39.140
And we have some great tools
with startup and shutdown

00:14:39.140 --> 00:14:41.220
scripts that allow
you to do this.

00:14:41.220 --> 00:14:43.820
So I'm going to have Ben
up on stage to demonstrate

00:14:43.820 --> 00:14:47.060
how their team takes
a code-driven approach

00:14:47.060 --> 00:14:51.350
to manage ID infrastructure
for alphabet companies who

00:14:51.350 --> 00:14:53.770
are running Windows
on Google Cloud.

00:14:53.770 --> 00:14:54.270
Ben?

00:15:00.945 --> 00:15:02.070
BEN MILLER: Thanks, Amruta.

00:15:04.860 --> 00:15:06.450
Hi, my name is Ben Miller.

00:15:06.450 --> 00:15:09.540
I work for Google's Mergers
and Acquisitions Infrastructure

00:15:09.540 --> 00:15:10.830
Services Team.

00:15:10.830 --> 00:15:13.290
And I'm here to talk to you
about our project GECCO, which

00:15:13.290 --> 00:15:19.320
is currently managing Windows
instances in Google Cloud.

00:15:19.320 --> 00:15:20.960
So what is GECCO?

00:15:20.960 --> 00:15:24.790
GECCO is code-driven, managed
IT infrastructure for alphabet

00:15:24.790 --> 00:15:26.820
entities and acquisitions.

00:15:26.820 --> 00:15:29.190
Alphabet entities are
separate companies.

00:15:29.190 --> 00:15:32.100
And so they often don't have
access to Google corporate IT

00:15:32.100 --> 00:15:33.540
infrastructure.

00:15:33.540 --> 00:15:36.630
Although GECCO needs customer
environments, excuse me,

00:15:36.630 --> 00:15:38.970
although each GECCO customer
needs an independent IT

00:15:38.970 --> 00:15:41.790
infrastructure for a
variety of reasons,

00:15:41.790 --> 00:15:44.310
we need a scalable
solution for management

00:15:44.310 --> 00:15:46.440
of those environments.

00:15:46.440 --> 00:15:49.320
We strive to achieve this
through services, policies,

00:15:49.320 --> 00:15:52.380
and standards that we can
define in code and deploy

00:15:52.380 --> 00:15:53.850
through automation.

00:15:53.850 --> 00:15:56.700
Our customers don't want
to spend time and energy

00:15:56.700 --> 00:15:58.620
reinventing the
wheels themselves,

00:15:58.620 --> 00:16:01.240
so they order their
wheels from us.

00:16:01.240 --> 00:16:04.590
GECCO environments contain
Linux and Windows workstations,

00:16:04.590 --> 00:16:07.500
excuse me, instances,
and use Active Directory

00:16:07.500 --> 00:16:10.294
for directory services.

00:16:10.294 --> 00:16:13.310
We provide Cloud-hosted
infrastructure services

00:16:13.310 --> 00:16:17.240
to GECCO customers, services
like Active Directory, Network

00:16:17.240 --> 00:16:19.430
Infrastructure and
Security Services,

00:16:19.430 --> 00:16:22.550
Central File Sharing,
Disaster Recovery.

00:16:22.550 --> 00:16:25.430
Our goal is to scalably
manage infrastructure services

00:16:25.430 --> 00:16:27.620
in per customer
deployments, following

00:16:27.620 --> 00:16:32.010
shared best practices, and using
a shared management framework.

00:16:32.010 --> 00:16:34.020
GECCO achieves this
in part by automating

00:16:34.020 --> 00:16:37.380
the deployment and configuration
of per customer GCE

00:16:37.380 --> 00:16:39.070
environments.

00:16:39.070 --> 00:16:42.640
Here's a very high level example
of what one might look like.

00:16:42.640 --> 00:16:44.650
A typical GECCO
environment is comprised

00:16:44.650 --> 00:16:47.860
of a customer-specific
GCE project connected

00:16:47.860 --> 00:16:52.180
to the customer's on-site
network via Cloud VPN.

00:16:52.180 --> 00:16:55.060
Typically, the project will
span multiple geographic regions

00:16:55.060 --> 00:16:56.170
for fault tolerance.

00:16:59.360 --> 00:17:01.340
Each GECCO service
is expressed in code

00:17:01.340 --> 00:17:04.910
that defines well-understood
and well-tested behaviors.

00:17:04.910 --> 00:17:07.339
This functional code does
not change from one customer

00:17:07.339 --> 00:17:08.720
to the next.

00:17:08.720 --> 00:17:10.579
Each GECCO customer
environment is

00:17:10.579 --> 00:17:13.400
defined in a customer-specific
repository, which

00:17:13.400 --> 00:17:14.930
contains structured
configuration

00:17:14.930 --> 00:17:17.420
files, certificates,
and other things that

00:17:17.420 --> 00:17:19.859
are specific to that customer.

00:17:19.859 --> 00:17:21.869
This repository
defines all aspects

00:17:21.869 --> 00:17:24.690
of the customer environment--
from host level configuration,

00:17:24.690 --> 00:17:27.839
to configuration parameters
that specialize the behavior

00:17:27.839 --> 00:17:30.270
of each GECCO service.

00:17:30.270 --> 00:17:32.590
This repository links
back to the main GECCO

00:17:32.590 --> 00:17:35.796
source, where all of the
functional code is maintained.

00:17:35.796 --> 00:17:37.920
Now, this link can point
to the most recent version

00:17:37.920 --> 00:17:39.420
of the GECCO code
where it can be

00:17:39.420 --> 00:17:41.210
pinned to a specific version.

00:17:41.210 --> 00:17:43.770
This simplifies
maintenance and upgrades

00:17:43.770 --> 00:17:46.320
as new versions of
GECCO and GECCO services

00:17:46.320 --> 00:17:47.890
can be rolled out over time.

00:17:51.380 --> 00:17:53.240
The separation between
functional code

00:17:53.240 --> 00:17:55.670
in GECCO and customer
configuration

00:17:55.670 --> 00:17:57.980
can be seen from the
beginning during the instance

00:17:57.980 --> 00:17:59.650
build process.

00:17:59.650 --> 00:18:02.300
GECCO instances are built
using a function that we define

00:18:02.300 --> 00:18:04.550
in our GECCO PowerShell module.

00:18:04.550 --> 00:18:07.700
The instance can be built
simply by referring to its name,

00:18:07.700 --> 00:18:09.710
and all of the necessary
details to build

00:18:09.710 --> 00:18:12.320
that instance will be
extracted from the customer's

00:18:12.320 --> 00:18:15.950
configuration.

00:18:15.950 --> 00:18:19.190
Now let me focus for a moment
on how Google Compute Engine has

00:18:19.190 --> 00:18:23.570
simplified Windows instance
building and configuration.

00:18:23.570 --> 00:18:27.160
First, let me call out the
Google Cloud PowerShell module.

00:18:27.160 --> 00:18:28.910
The folks in Google
Cloud have provided us

00:18:28.910 --> 00:18:31.190
with some fantastic
tools to manage

00:18:31.190 --> 00:18:33.950
GCE resources using PowerShell.

00:18:33.950 --> 00:18:36.560
I'll be using commandlets from
this module to demonstrate

00:18:36.560 --> 00:18:39.460
how we build Windows
infrastructure.

00:18:39.460 --> 00:18:41.440
Now let's talk about
the build image.

00:18:41.440 --> 00:18:43.660
The Windows Server
image provided by GCE

00:18:43.660 --> 00:18:46.690
is kept up-to-date for you
with the latest hot fixes,

00:18:46.690 --> 00:18:49.280
so you don't have to
maintain that yourself.

00:18:49.280 --> 00:18:51.280
When you boot a Windows
image for the first time

00:18:51.280 --> 00:18:54.160
in Google Cloud, it boots
into the sysprep-specialize

00:18:54.160 --> 00:18:55.727
configuration pass.

00:18:55.727 --> 00:18:58.060
And for those of you who are
less familiar with sysprep,

00:18:58.060 --> 00:19:00.630
what this means is that the
first time a Windows instance

00:19:00.630 --> 00:19:03.340
is spun up in your
project, it runs

00:19:03.340 --> 00:19:05.260
a series of
specialization tasks that

00:19:05.260 --> 00:19:07.970
finalize the Windows install.

00:19:07.970 --> 00:19:11.360
GCE gives you several options
to inject custom scripts

00:19:11.360 --> 00:19:14.740
into this process without
rebuilding the image.

00:19:14.740 --> 00:19:17.420
And one of these options is
to leverage the GCE metadata

00:19:17.420 --> 00:19:20.630
service to store an
instance-specific script that

00:19:20.630 --> 00:19:23.080
will be executed during
the specialized pass.

00:19:23.080 --> 00:19:25.020
And that's what we do.

00:19:25.020 --> 00:19:27.420
Note that this is in addition
to the startup and shutdown

00:19:27.420 --> 00:19:29.550
scripts you can also
define in metadata

00:19:29.550 --> 00:19:33.910
that will run at each startup
and shutdown of the instance.

00:19:33.910 --> 00:19:36.600
GECCO uses sysprep-specialize
as an opportunity

00:19:36.600 --> 00:19:39.937
to inject a very simple script
to bootstrap the GECCO build

00:19:39.937 --> 00:19:41.020
and configuration process.

00:19:52.781 --> 00:19:53.280
OK.

00:19:53.280 --> 00:19:55.090
Can we switch to the
demo computer, please?

00:19:59.681 --> 00:20:00.180
OK.

00:20:00.180 --> 00:20:02.221
The code that you will
see here is representative

00:20:02.221 --> 00:20:04.482
of the process of the
GECCO Project employees,

00:20:04.482 --> 00:20:06.440
but it is, of course,
not exactly the same code

00:20:06.440 --> 00:20:08.910
that we use in production.

00:20:08.910 --> 00:20:10.859
Here we have a short
script that will be used

00:20:10.859 --> 00:20:12.025
to build a Windows instance.

00:20:14.850 --> 00:20:17.220
What we've done here is
reference the current GCE

00:20:17.220 --> 00:20:21.180
Windows Server 2016
Core image, populate

00:20:21.180 --> 00:20:24.390
the appropriate metadata
with our bootstrap script,

00:20:24.390 --> 00:20:28.350
and call Add GCE instance
to create the new machine.

00:20:28.350 --> 00:20:31.770
This commandlet is part of the
Google Cloud PowerShell module.

00:20:31.770 --> 00:20:34.500
As you can see, you
can also add a startup

00:20:34.500 --> 00:20:37.062
and/or shut down
script at the same time

00:20:37.062 --> 00:20:38.520
when you are creating
the instance.

00:20:41.092 --> 00:20:42.550
So while this runs,
let me show you

00:20:42.550 --> 00:20:46.090
how instance metadata becomes
a sysprep-specialize script.

00:20:51.940 --> 00:20:53.950
Using the get GCE
instance commandlet

00:20:53.950 --> 00:20:56.260
you can retrieve an
object that describes

00:20:56.260 --> 00:21:01.140
all of the attributes
of your GCE instances.

00:21:01.140 --> 00:21:03.120
Now, here I've drilled
down specifically just

00:21:03.120 --> 00:21:05.280
to list the instance metadata.

00:21:05.280 --> 00:21:07.390
You can see that the
startup, shutdown,

00:21:07.390 --> 00:21:10.590
and sysprep-specialize
keys have been populated

00:21:10.590 --> 00:21:14.120
with the values we provided when
the instance was first created.

00:21:14.120 --> 00:21:16.500
And when a Windows
instance is first created,

00:21:16.500 --> 00:21:20.250
the GCE agent on
that machine looks

00:21:20.250 --> 00:21:23.500
for the sysprep-specialize key
in the GCP metadata service.

00:21:23.500 --> 00:21:25.960
And if it exists,
it executes it.

00:21:25.960 --> 00:21:27.390
This is a really
low-friction way

00:21:27.390 --> 00:21:29.700
of customizing your
Windows instance builds.

00:21:32.570 --> 00:21:34.310
As the script
executes, its output

00:21:34.310 --> 00:21:36.549
is mirrored to the
instances serial port.

00:21:36.549 --> 00:21:38.840
You already saw some of that
in Amruta's demonstration,

00:21:38.840 --> 00:21:41.740
but I want to show you how
this works in PowerShell.

00:21:41.740 --> 00:21:44.440
You can observe the serial
output of your GCP instances

00:21:44.440 --> 00:21:47.710
by using the Cloud console or
by using the get GCE instance

00:21:47.710 --> 00:21:48.670
commandlet.

00:21:51.310 --> 00:21:54.610
You can see low-level output
of the instance boot process,

00:21:54.610 --> 00:21:57.855
as well as other messages that
are logged by the GCE agent.

00:21:57.855 --> 00:21:59.797
And if I do a little
bit of quick filtering,

00:21:59.797 --> 00:22:01.630
we should see just the
output of the scripts

00:22:01.630 --> 00:22:03.912
that we defined in metadata.

00:22:10.126 --> 00:22:11.500
The output is
truncated somewhat,

00:22:11.500 --> 00:22:13.041
but you can see what
I've done there.

00:22:16.140 --> 00:22:19.080
As the script executes-- oh,
excuse me, one more slide.

00:22:19.080 --> 00:22:19.930
There we go.

00:22:19.930 --> 00:22:20.430
OK.

00:22:20.430 --> 00:22:21.930
Let's go back to
the slides, please.

00:22:25.110 --> 00:22:28.635
So to recap, using
Google Cloud PowerShell

00:22:28.635 --> 00:22:31.010
we were able to reference a
pre-built Windows Server Core

00:22:31.010 --> 00:22:33.620
image, defined
startup, shutdown,

00:22:33.620 --> 00:22:38.390
and sysprep-specialize scripts,
and create a GCE instance.

00:22:38.390 --> 00:22:41.540
We were also able to monitor
the progress of the instances

00:22:41.540 --> 00:22:43.470
build using serial output.

00:22:43.470 --> 00:22:47.180
And we did all of this
from within PowerShell.

00:22:47.180 --> 00:22:50.580
Now, for GECCO, now that
we have built an instance,

00:22:50.580 --> 00:22:54.710
how will it be
configured and managed?

00:22:54.710 --> 00:22:57.800
In a nutshell, GECCO uses
customer configuration files

00:22:57.800 --> 00:23:01.130
to drive PowerShell Desired
State Configuration.

00:23:01.130 --> 00:23:02.460
How does this work?

00:23:02.460 --> 00:23:06.110
Well, each GECCO environment
has a configuration master.

00:23:06.110 --> 00:23:09.170
In DSC terms, this is
called a pull server.

00:23:09.170 --> 00:23:12.410
The config master iterates over
the list of all GECCO instances

00:23:12.410 --> 00:23:14.870
defined in the customer config.

00:23:14.870 --> 00:23:18.620
Based on the roles and services
associated with each instance,

00:23:18.620 --> 00:23:20.570
templates are merged
together to construct

00:23:20.570 --> 00:23:23.630
the full configuration
for the instance.

00:23:23.630 --> 00:23:25.130
Within each
configuration template

00:23:25.130 --> 00:23:27.410
are DSC resources,
whose attributes

00:23:27.410 --> 00:23:30.230
are populated using
properties that

00:23:30.230 --> 00:23:34.420
are stored also in the
customer configuration file.

00:23:34.420 --> 00:23:37.480
The output of this process is
a compiled DSC configuration

00:23:37.480 --> 00:23:40.390
for each instance which
the pull server will host.

00:23:40.390 --> 00:23:42.280
And on a schedule,
managed instances

00:23:42.280 --> 00:23:46.280
will pull their new
configuration and apply them.

00:23:46.280 --> 00:23:48.760
They will report back to
the config master with data

00:23:48.760 --> 00:23:50.710
on what changes were
made and whether or not

00:23:50.710 --> 00:23:53.420
the configuration was
applied successfully.

00:23:53.420 --> 00:23:55.720
Now, this is not a
one-time operation.

00:23:55.720 --> 00:23:57.550
Managed instances
will continually

00:23:57.550 --> 00:24:01.360
re-evaluate their configuration
and ensure that the desired

00:24:01.360 --> 00:24:04.870
configuration is applied.

00:24:04.870 --> 00:24:07.850
So our instances have
been built and configured.

00:24:07.850 --> 00:24:11.080
How do we make changes to
a customer environment?

00:24:11.080 --> 00:24:13.990
Our management pipeline is
driven by open source projects.

00:24:13.990 --> 00:24:16.150
We use Gerrit for
Git-based code review

00:24:16.150 --> 00:24:18.820
and Jenkins for
continuous integration.

00:24:18.820 --> 00:24:20.690
GECCO strives to be a
management framework

00:24:20.690 --> 00:24:22.800
with strong accountability
for changes.

00:24:22.800 --> 00:24:25.830
So how do we achieve this?

00:24:25.830 --> 00:24:28.550
First, a GECCO admin
submits a change for review.

00:24:28.550 --> 00:24:30.770
Now, this does not alter
the customer environment,

00:24:30.770 --> 00:24:33.080
but it's a proposed change.

00:24:33.080 --> 00:24:35.660
This change can be anything
from a new DNS record,

00:24:35.660 --> 00:24:39.350
to a change to Group
Policy, to firewall rules,

00:24:39.350 --> 00:24:41.200
just to name a few examples.

00:24:41.200 --> 00:24:44.160
Now, this proposed change needs
approval from two sources.

00:24:44.160 --> 00:24:46.580
First, our Jenkins
instance will automatically

00:24:46.580 --> 00:24:49.490
run unit and integration tests.

00:24:49.490 --> 00:24:52.760
And second, another member
of the GECCO admin team

00:24:52.760 --> 00:24:56.460
has to review the commit
and provide final approval.

00:24:56.460 --> 00:24:58.340
Now, finally, once
all this is done,

00:24:58.340 --> 00:25:00.860
the configuration master
will recompile instance

00:25:00.860 --> 00:25:02.990
configurations
based on the change,

00:25:02.990 --> 00:25:05.925
and the GECCO instances
will pick up those changes.

00:25:05.925 --> 00:25:08.990
Now, this management pipeline
is the core of GECCO.

00:25:08.990 --> 00:25:09.636
But why?

00:25:09.636 --> 00:25:12.260
Why did we take this approach to
systems administration instead

00:25:12.260 --> 00:25:16.640
of managing things in a
more traditional fashion?

00:25:16.640 --> 00:25:20.930
By defining our architecture
in code and config,

00:25:20.930 --> 00:25:23.780
we can have strong assurance
that the infrastructure is

00:25:23.780 --> 00:25:26.720
built and operates as designed.

00:25:26.720 --> 00:25:28.790
When we implement
best practices,

00:25:28.790 --> 00:25:31.490
we are able to implement
across all of our customers.

00:25:31.490 --> 00:25:34.070
And by standardizing the
services that we deploy,

00:25:34.070 --> 00:25:35.830
and the way in which
we deploy them,

00:25:35.830 --> 00:25:38.570
we are able to manage
many customer environments

00:25:38.570 --> 00:25:42.410
using the same set of
management tools and procedures.

00:25:42.410 --> 00:25:44.030
All of this allows
us to scale support

00:25:44.030 --> 00:25:47.660
for additional customers without
having to also linearly scale

00:25:47.660 --> 00:25:51.150
the size of our team.

00:25:51.150 --> 00:25:54.000
But why do we choose
to build GECCO on GCE?

00:25:54.000 --> 00:25:56.610
Well, first of all,
we take a lot of value

00:25:56.610 --> 00:26:01.020
from GCE's specific support
for Windows as a platform.

00:26:01.020 --> 00:26:04.110
Custom instance
sizing and simple

00:26:04.110 --> 00:26:06.330
hosted Windows
licensing makes it easy

00:26:06.330 --> 00:26:10.550
for us to predict invoice
costs per customer.

00:26:10.550 --> 00:26:14.540
Building Windows VMs on GCE
is really fast and simple

00:26:14.540 --> 00:26:15.890
to automate.

00:26:15.890 --> 00:26:18.930
And Google Cloud PowerShell
is a huge win for us,

00:26:18.930 --> 00:26:21.500
allowing us to automate GCE
using the same management

00:26:21.500 --> 00:26:25.580
framework that we use to manage
our Windows infrastructure.

00:26:25.580 --> 00:26:28.010
Now, finally, as a
customer of Google Cloud,

00:26:28.010 --> 00:26:31.310
the single biggest win is
the performance, stability,

00:26:31.310 --> 00:26:33.230
and security of GCP.

00:26:33.230 --> 00:26:37.280
It allows our team to focus
on just those challenges that

00:26:37.280 --> 00:26:39.300
are ours to solve.

00:26:39.300 --> 00:26:41.300
Now, let me hand the
time back to Amruta,

00:26:41.300 --> 00:26:44.220
who's going to talk about
container support for Windows

00:26:44.220 --> 00:26:46.750
Workloads.

00:26:46.750 --> 00:26:49.820
AMRUTA GULANIKAR:
Thank you, Ben.

00:26:49.820 --> 00:26:55.760
So you saw a great demonstration
of how you can configure

00:26:55.760 --> 00:26:58.500
Windows infrastructure on GCE.

00:26:58.500 --> 00:27:01.700
A question I often get from
customers more recently

00:27:01.700 --> 00:27:05.330
is, how do you deploy your
Windows-based applications

00:27:05.330 --> 00:27:06.210
using containers?

00:27:06.210 --> 00:27:08.210
And the answer is there
are many different paths

00:27:08.210 --> 00:27:10.190
that you can take on GCP.

00:27:10.190 --> 00:27:13.460
You can stand up a Windows
server VM, Windows Server

00:27:13.460 --> 00:27:16.830
2016, which is the first version
to support Docker and Windows

00:27:16.830 --> 00:27:20.420
Server containers, and run your
Windows containers on that.

00:27:20.420 --> 00:27:22.940
We also have support for Nano
Silver, which is coming soon,

00:27:22.940 --> 00:27:25.070
which will have a much
smaller footprint.

00:27:25.070 --> 00:27:28.640
You can choose to
have ASP.NET Core apps

00:27:28.640 --> 00:27:31.400
and run them in either Windows
Silver containers or Linux

00:27:31.400 --> 00:27:33.090
containers.

00:27:33.090 --> 00:27:36.139
You have options when you
want to manage orchestration

00:27:36.139 --> 00:27:36.930
of your containers.

00:27:36.930 --> 00:27:39.190
And you can either choose
Kubernetes or Google Compute

00:27:39.190 --> 00:27:40.380
Engine.

00:27:40.380 --> 00:27:43.260
So today, if you have
an ASP.NET Core app,

00:27:43.260 --> 00:27:45.030
you can run it on
a Linux container

00:27:45.030 --> 00:27:47.340
on Google Container Engine.

00:27:47.340 --> 00:27:51.476
We are also contributing to the
open source special interest

00:27:51.476 --> 00:27:53.100
group that I mentioned,
which is tasked

00:27:53.100 --> 00:27:58.240
with bringing Windows containers
support to Kubernetes.

00:27:58.240 --> 00:28:00.550
And if you want an even
more managed solution,

00:28:00.550 --> 00:28:01.350
then you can--

00:28:01.350 --> 00:28:05.140
we just launched
support for ASP.NET Core

00:28:05.140 --> 00:28:08.600
on App Engine Flex, or App
Engine Flexible Environments.

00:28:08.600 --> 00:28:11.230
So you can have multiple
different options.

00:28:11.230 --> 00:28:14.380
Now, in order to demonstrate the
great work that the open source

00:28:14.380 --> 00:28:16.540
community has been doing
for Windows containers

00:28:16.540 --> 00:28:20.100
and Kubernetes, I'd like
to call up Michael on stage

00:28:20.100 --> 00:28:21.143
to talk more about that.

00:28:26.062 --> 00:28:27.520
MICHAEL MICHAEL:
Thank you, Amruta.

00:28:27.520 --> 00:28:28.300
Hi, everybody.

00:28:28.300 --> 00:28:31.420
So I'm here representing
the Special Interest

00:28:31.420 --> 00:28:33.010
Group for Windows
Server containers

00:28:33.010 --> 00:28:34.780
from the Kubernetes community.

00:28:34.780 --> 00:28:37.360
So a quick show of hands, how
many of you guys have used

00:28:37.360 --> 00:28:40.600
Kubernetes so far, whether
on-premise or in a Cloud

00:28:40.600 --> 00:28:42.710
provider, like Google Cloud?

00:28:42.710 --> 00:28:44.140
All right, a few of you guys.

00:28:44.140 --> 00:28:46.450
So one of the most
important things

00:28:46.450 --> 00:28:49.600
is if you are using
Kubernetes, it

00:28:49.600 --> 00:28:52.510
is one of the best
tools out there

00:28:52.510 --> 00:28:54.880
to be able to
[AUDIO OUT], manage,

00:28:54.880 --> 00:28:57.160
deploy containers at scale.

00:28:57.160 --> 00:28:59.090
It really makes life simple.

00:28:59.090 --> 00:29:01.810
So if you are deploying
containers today

00:29:01.810 --> 00:29:06.550
in traditional virtual machines,
or whether that's on-premise,

00:29:06.550 --> 00:29:09.610
or in a Cloud provider, it's
hard to actually make sure

00:29:09.610 --> 00:29:12.370
what image are you using,
control the workflow,

00:29:12.370 --> 00:29:14.170
whether that's a
continuous integration

00:29:14.170 --> 00:29:16.060
or a continuous
deployment system,

00:29:16.060 --> 00:29:17.770
and being able to scale those.

00:29:17.770 --> 00:29:20.660
With Kubernetes, you
have a complete platform

00:29:20.660 --> 00:29:23.500
that manages the end-to-end
lifecycle of your containers.

00:29:23.500 --> 00:29:26.260
From the point where you
define where the application

00:29:26.260 --> 00:29:28.030
you want to run
into the container,

00:29:28.030 --> 00:29:29.590
to defining the
networking for it,

00:29:29.590 --> 00:29:33.070
being able to scale it to the
hundreds or thousands of nodes,

00:29:33.070 --> 00:29:34.900
and then being able
to version that and go

00:29:34.900 --> 00:29:38.110
from version 1 to version
2, and so on and so forth.

00:29:38.110 --> 00:29:42.190
So within the community, which
has been running for a while

00:29:42.190 --> 00:29:44.330
now, one thing was lacking.

00:29:44.330 --> 00:29:46.550
And that was Windows
Server support.

00:29:46.550 --> 00:29:49.450
So the SIG Windows
team was tasked

00:29:49.450 --> 00:29:52.480
to go and bring Windows
Server support to Kubernetes,

00:29:52.480 --> 00:29:55.180
and more importantly, to be
able to run Windows Server

00:29:55.180 --> 00:29:56.500
containers.

00:29:56.500 --> 00:30:00.610
What that will give us is one
of the best cross-platform,

00:30:00.610 --> 00:30:02.710
heterogeneous cluster monitors.

00:30:02.710 --> 00:30:05.950
And it would allow us to expand
the types of applications

00:30:05.950 --> 00:30:07.510
that can run on Kubernetes.

00:30:07.510 --> 00:30:10.600
So before, you used to have
applications that will run

00:30:10.600 --> 00:30:12.490
on the Linux operating system.

00:30:12.490 --> 00:30:14.800
By introducing Windows
Server containers,

00:30:14.800 --> 00:30:17.860
now you can run
IIS-based applications.

00:30:17.860 --> 00:30:20.320
You can run .net
Core applications.

00:30:20.320 --> 00:30:24.380
You can run Windows services
and any other application

00:30:24.380 --> 00:30:26.680
that can on the Windows OS.

00:30:26.680 --> 00:30:29.670
So that's a tremendous
amount of change introduced

00:30:29.670 --> 00:30:31.660
to Kubernetes now
with the ability

00:30:31.660 --> 00:30:35.110
to run all these
extra workloads.

00:30:35.110 --> 00:30:38.620
And all of our work here in
the Special Interest Group

00:30:38.620 --> 00:30:40.180
is really based
on top of the work

00:30:40.180 --> 00:30:43.330
that Microsoft has done to
both introduce Windows Server

00:30:43.330 --> 00:30:46.172
containers to their
operating system

00:30:46.172 --> 00:30:48.130
as well as a lot of the
networking enhancements

00:30:48.130 --> 00:30:49.324
that have been made.

00:30:49.324 --> 00:30:51.490
There's a few companies
that I want to call out here

00:30:51.490 --> 00:30:54.400
that have been doing a
lot of work in the group,

00:30:54.400 --> 00:30:57.100
and that's Apprenda,
where I'm coming from.

00:30:57.100 --> 00:31:01.990
There's CloudBase, Google,
Microsoft, and Red Hat as well.

00:31:01.990 --> 00:31:06.300
All these guys are contributing
to make this a reality.

00:31:06.300 --> 00:31:09.790
From a status
perspective, we have Alpha

00:31:09.790 --> 00:31:12.040
support in Kubernetes today.

00:31:12.040 --> 00:31:15.520
So you can go and download
our binaries and try this out.

00:31:15.520 --> 00:31:18.150
And with the release
of Kubernetes

00:31:18.150 --> 00:31:21.390
that will come towards the
end of Q2 of this year,

00:31:21.390 --> 00:31:22.210
we'll go in beta.

00:31:27.250 --> 00:31:29.680
So let's take a look at the
architecture really quickly.

00:31:29.680 --> 00:31:31.800
One of the things that
we wanted to make sure

00:31:31.800 --> 00:31:34.750
is that we kept the simplicity
of Kubernetes and Docker

00:31:34.750 --> 00:31:36.090
as it exists today.

00:31:36.090 --> 00:31:38.910
So on the left-hand side,
you have the existing Docker

00:31:38.910 --> 00:31:42.120
architecture for
Linux on Kubernetes.

00:31:42.120 --> 00:31:45.270
And you'll see that you have
the Docker images, the engine,

00:31:45.270 --> 00:31:48.000
and then you have a shared
Kernel down at the bottom,

00:31:48.000 --> 00:31:51.300
where you can have one or
more containers running on top

00:31:51.300 --> 00:31:53.450
of the same container host.

00:31:53.450 --> 00:31:57.690
That's the compute unit
that's running your ecosystem.

00:31:57.690 --> 00:32:00.450
And on the right side, when
we started architecting things

00:32:00.450 --> 00:32:03.170
for the Windows side,
it was very advantageous

00:32:03.170 --> 00:32:06.030
that Docker now runs
on Windows Server.

00:32:06.030 --> 00:32:08.700
And it has a very similar model
to what they are in Linux,

00:32:08.700 --> 00:32:11.615
so we maintain the parity
of having the Docker

00:32:11.615 --> 00:32:12.492
images on the node.

00:32:12.492 --> 00:32:13.950
You have your Docker
engine daemon,

00:32:13.950 --> 00:32:17.220
which is what we are
talking to as Kubernetes,

00:32:17.220 --> 00:32:20.100
and then having one or
more containers running

00:32:20.100 --> 00:32:22.260
on top of a shared Kernel.

00:32:22.260 --> 00:32:25.110
I would like to point out
that Windows Server supports

00:32:25.110 --> 00:32:28.260
two types of containers today.

00:32:28.260 --> 00:32:29.970
One of it is Windows
Server containers,

00:32:29.970 --> 00:32:31.530
where you have a shared Kernel.

00:32:31.530 --> 00:32:34.050
And it also supports
Hyper-V containers,

00:32:34.050 --> 00:32:37.290
where you have kernel
mode isolation as well.

00:32:37.290 --> 00:32:39.540
Right now we have been
predominantly working

00:32:39.540 --> 00:32:41.430
on Windows Server
containers, but there's

00:32:41.430 --> 00:32:44.220
nothing that prevents us from
supporting Hyper-V containers

00:32:44.220 --> 00:32:46.070
as well as we go towards beta.

00:32:49.620 --> 00:32:51.720
With the alpha
support that we had,

00:32:51.720 --> 00:32:53.640
this is what our
architecture looked like.

00:32:53.640 --> 00:32:55.410
So you have the
Physical Internet

00:32:55.410 --> 00:32:56.880
coming up from the top.

00:32:56.880 --> 00:32:59.130
And then you have the
kubelet and the kube-proxy,

00:32:59.130 --> 00:33:02.850
which are the two components of
Kubernetes that kind of drive

00:33:02.850 --> 00:33:04.420
the core functionality.

00:33:04.420 --> 00:33:07.800
And then you have an
abstraction called

00:33:07.800 --> 00:33:12.870
a pod, which is the lowest unit
of deployment in Kubernetes

00:33:12.870 --> 00:33:14.940
that can contain one
or more containers.

00:33:14.940 --> 00:33:17.460
Think of a pod as
a microservice,

00:33:17.460 --> 00:33:20.040
for example, that
can be implemented

00:33:20.040 --> 00:33:22.500
inside one or more containers.

00:33:22.500 --> 00:33:26.700
So with the alpha support, this
is what it would look like.

00:33:26.700 --> 00:33:30.520
And we went ahead and completely
rewrote the networking stack

00:33:30.520 --> 00:33:34.010
of Kubernetes for
Windows Server containers

00:33:34.010 --> 00:33:37.410
as we march towards beta
to eliminate completely

00:33:37.410 --> 00:33:41.610
the kube-proxy and use OVN
and OVS as the networking

00:33:41.610 --> 00:33:43.660
backbone for the containers.

00:33:43.660 --> 00:33:46.650
This provided us a tremendous
amount of advantages.

00:33:46.650 --> 00:33:49.020
And it allows us as we
move towards the future

00:33:49.020 --> 00:33:51.780
to support things
like network overlays

00:33:51.780 --> 00:33:53.880
and be able to do
a network namespace

00:33:53.880 --> 00:33:58.270
isolation for containers that
spans both Windows and Linux.

00:33:58.270 --> 00:34:01.430
So imagine this, you'll be able
to have a single application

00:34:01.430 --> 00:34:04.830
that has containers running
on Linux and on Windows, both

00:34:04.830 --> 00:34:06.960
of them in a single
network namespace,

00:34:06.960 --> 00:34:12.539
communicating with each other
in a secure and scalable manner.

00:34:12.539 --> 00:34:14.330
So let's switch here
to a demo and show you

00:34:14.330 --> 00:34:18.290
some of this work in real time.

00:34:18.290 --> 00:34:20.710
And what you're looking at
here is the Google Cloud

00:34:20.710 --> 00:34:21.520
as the k-shell.

00:34:21.520 --> 00:34:24.420
So all of our components here
are running in Google Cloud.

00:34:24.420 --> 00:34:26.170
So I'm going to go
ahead and create an SSH

00:34:26.170 --> 00:34:29.780
session to my Kubernetes
master component.

00:34:29.780 --> 00:34:33.590
So I will go ahead and
change the resolution here

00:34:33.590 --> 00:34:36.209
so you guys can see
that fairly well.

00:34:42.756 --> 00:34:44.630
All right, so the first
thing I'm going to do

00:34:44.630 --> 00:34:47.780
is we're going to go ahead
and run as administrator here.

00:34:47.780 --> 00:34:50.179
And I'm going to run kubectl.

00:34:50.179 --> 00:34:52.670
This is the command line
interface with Kubernetes.

00:34:52.670 --> 00:34:55.280
And I'm going to go ahead
and get all the nodes that we

00:34:55.280 --> 00:34:56.335
have in the system here.

00:34:56.335 --> 00:34:57.710
And notice that
I have two nodes.

00:34:57.710 --> 00:35:00.950
I have a master component
that's running on Linux.

00:35:00.950 --> 00:35:06.452
And it contains all of the
core pieces to run Kubernetes.

00:35:06.452 --> 00:35:07.910
And then the second
thing I have is

00:35:07.910 --> 00:35:12.290
I have a worker node that's
running Windows Server that is

00:35:12.290 --> 00:35:15.770
able to host pods on Windows.

00:35:15.770 --> 00:35:18.800
So if I check if I
have any pods here,

00:35:18.800 --> 00:35:21.350
you'll notice that I don't have
any pods deployed right now.

00:35:21.350 --> 00:35:25.420
So there's no resources
deployed in the system.

00:35:25.420 --> 00:35:28.880
To demonstrate here some of
the capabilities of our node,

00:35:28.880 --> 00:35:32.600
let's go ahead and
describe the Windows node.

00:35:32.600 --> 00:35:37.000
And notice that its
architecture is AMD64.

00:35:37.000 --> 00:35:39.950
It's running the same kubelet
and kube-proxy version

00:35:39.950 --> 00:35:43.000
that the rest of my Kubernetes
environment is running.

00:35:43.000 --> 00:35:46.390
And it has the same
type of details

00:35:46.390 --> 00:35:48.760
in terms of the CPU
performance metrics,

00:35:48.760 --> 00:35:52.310
the memory metrics, and all
the other details, like labels,

00:35:52.310 --> 00:35:53.680
that Kubernetes has.

00:35:53.680 --> 00:35:56.920
So if I run this
command here now,

00:35:56.920 --> 00:36:00.820
I'll be able to show you the
labels that are identifying

00:36:00.820 --> 00:36:02.160
each node in my system.

00:36:02.160 --> 00:36:04.060
So notice that my master
component is running

00:36:04.060 --> 00:36:06.400
Linux with AMD64 architecture.

00:36:06.400 --> 00:36:09.340
And now I have my
worker in Windows node

00:36:09.340 --> 00:36:11.800
that's running the
Windows operating system.

00:36:11.800 --> 00:36:13.630
And it's labeled accordingly.

00:36:13.630 --> 00:36:15.670
And now this label is
important, because that's

00:36:15.670 --> 00:36:20.190
how we're going to select pods
to be attached to that node.

00:36:20.190 --> 00:36:23.050
So when you're deploying
containers in Kubernetes,

00:36:23.050 --> 00:36:24.850
you get to tell
Kubernetes where would you

00:36:24.850 --> 00:36:26.380
like those to be deployed.

00:36:26.380 --> 00:36:29.040
Think of this as a
simple deployment policy.

00:36:29.040 --> 00:36:31.960
And we'll use the labels
that are identifying

00:36:31.960 --> 00:36:34.350
each one of our nodes.

00:36:34.350 --> 00:36:36.620
So let's get started here.

00:36:36.620 --> 00:36:39.304
Let's go ahead and
deploy some containers.

00:36:43.885 --> 00:36:45.760
So the first containers
I'm going to go ahead

00:36:45.760 --> 00:36:50.560
and deploy is the first
tier of my application.

00:36:50.560 --> 00:36:56.830
And this is a Redis cache
that will form the data

00:36:56.830 --> 00:36:58.910
part of my application.

00:36:58.910 --> 00:37:01.150
So my application is going
to be a microservice that

00:37:01.150 --> 00:37:04.900
has three components-- a
redis-slave, a redis-master,

00:37:04.900 --> 00:37:08.710
as well as a guestbook
Go application-- running

00:37:08.710 --> 00:37:10.590
all of these components
running on Windows.

00:37:10.590 --> 00:37:13.006
But there's nothing preventing
me from running one of them

00:37:13.006 --> 00:37:14.750
on Linux if I so choose.

00:37:14.750 --> 00:37:17.470
So we want to have them
deploy the redis-master.

00:37:17.470 --> 00:37:21.760
So I can go ahead and run
my pods command again.

00:37:21.760 --> 00:37:24.520
And now notice that I
have a single instance

00:37:24.520 --> 00:37:26.560
of my redis-master running here.

00:37:26.560 --> 00:37:34.670
So if I run my command
kubectl logs and point it

00:37:34.670 --> 00:37:39.560
to the redis-master, notice
does that we have redis-3.0.504

00:37:39.560 --> 00:37:41.300
running inside Windows.

00:37:41.300 --> 00:37:44.210
So let's go ahead and
create the slave now

00:37:44.210 --> 00:37:47.210
that will communicate
with the master pod

00:37:47.210 --> 00:37:50.140
to complete the second
tier of our application.

00:37:50.140 --> 00:37:52.210
And one of the things
I want to show you

00:37:52.210 --> 00:37:53.940
is what does that look like.

00:37:53.940 --> 00:37:56.540
So let's go ahead and
do that and look at one

00:37:56.540 --> 00:37:59.330
of the Redis components here.

00:38:03.740 --> 00:38:07.210
So this is a deployment
specification on Kubernetes.

00:38:07.210 --> 00:38:09.760
So the pod, that abstraction
I mentioned earlier,

00:38:09.760 --> 00:38:12.590
is defined using a
specification like this.

00:38:12.590 --> 00:38:14.450
So in this deployment
specification

00:38:14.450 --> 00:38:18.040
I'm indicating that my
name is redis-slave.

00:38:18.040 --> 00:38:26.110
And I want to use this specific
image from the Docker hub.

00:38:26.110 --> 00:38:29.260
It's called
redis-slave:3.0-nanoserver.

00:38:29.260 --> 00:38:31.720
So it's using the nanoserver OS.

00:38:31.720 --> 00:38:36.340
And it's basically exposing
port 6379 over TCP.

00:38:36.340 --> 00:38:38.590
And the last
important thing here

00:38:38.590 --> 00:38:41.560
is the fact that
the node selector

00:38:41.560 --> 00:38:43.660
is targeting Windows Server.

00:38:43.660 --> 00:38:49.450
So now that we did this, if we
exit out of the component here

00:38:49.450 --> 00:38:52.640
and we run our
get-pods command again,

00:38:52.640 --> 00:38:58.900
notice now that I have two
slave redis-slave pods up

00:38:58.900 --> 00:38:59.920
and running.

00:38:59.920 --> 00:39:03.910
And they have a very distinct
IP address for each one of them.

00:39:03.910 --> 00:39:06.310
And that's basically--
and they're all

00:39:06.310 --> 00:39:08.410
running on Windows Server now.

00:39:08.410 --> 00:39:14.440
So if I go back and check
the logs for my master,

00:39:14.440 --> 00:39:17.320
notice now that the master
was able to communicate

00:39:17.320 --> 00:39:20.050
with the slave at port 6379.

00:39:20.050 --> 00:39:23.230
And that's the same port that
I identified in my description

00:39:23.230 --> 00:39:27.250
telling it, go ahead and find
each other using that port.

00:39:27.250 --> 00:39:30.250
So now we have two tiers of
our application up and running.

00:39:30.250 --> 00:39:31.240
We have a redis-slave.

00:39:31.240 --> 00:39:32.680
We have a redis-master.

00:39:32.680 --> 00:39:35.090
They are all able to find
each other and communicate.

00:39:35.090 --> 00:39:36.650
And they're all
running in Windows.

00:39:36.650 --> 00:39:41.930
So let's go ahead now
and deploy the last piece

00:39:41.930 --> 00:39:47.800
of our application, which is a
guestbook application written

00:39:47.800 --> 00:39:48.515
in Go.

00:39:55.170 --> 00:39:58.030
Notice now that we
created a single instance

00:39:58.030 --> 00:39:59.650
of our guestbook application.

00:39:59.650 --> 00:40:03.280
And I can actually go
ahead and connect to it.

00:40:03.280 --> 00:40:08.940
So I can run kubectlexec-it
and provide the guestbook node

00:40:08.940 --> 00:40:10.590
here.

00:40:10.590 --> 00:40:13.740
And I'm gonna run PowerShell.

00:40:13.740 --> 00:40:16.320
So now we have a
partial execution

00:40:16.320 --> 00:40:19.560
inside the container that's
running our guestbook

00:40:19.560 --> 00:40:20.100
application.

00:40:20.100 --> 00:40:23.050
So I can run IP config here.

00:40:23.050 --> 00:40:24.830
And I get an IP address.

00:40:24.830 --> 00:40:27.010
And that's the IP
address on my container.

00:40:27.010 --> 00:40:29.476
And I can even actually
ping the outside world.

00:40:29.476 --> 00:40:30.850
You might not
think this is much,

00:40:30.850 --> 00:40:33.880
but it took us a long
time to be able to enable

00:40:33.880 --> 00:40:37.180
internet access inside a
container using the OVN overlay

00:40:37.180 --> 00:40:37.960
network.

00:40:37.960 --> 00:40:41.890
So we had to introduce
things like a gateway node,

00:40:41.890 --> 00:40:44.020
and be able to
route the traffic,

00:40:44.020 --> 00:40:47.960
create the correct inter-pod and
inter-container connectivity.

00:40:47.960 --> 00:40:50.620
So even though this is simple,
and we all take internet access

00:40:50.620 --> 00:40:54.560
for granted, this was a lot
of work to make that happen.

00:40:54.560 --> 00:40:56.710
So now we actually
have our pods.

00:40:56.710 --> 00:41:04.330
We have the service as well
and three different sets

00:41:04.330 --> 00:41:07.260
of pods that comprise
this specific service.

00:41:07.260 --> 00:41:10.020
So let's go ahead and describe
our service in Kubernetes.

00:41:14.310 --> 00:41:17.110
And just a few details
here about our service

00:41:17.110 --> 00:41:19.770
that were taken out
of the specification.

00:41:19.770 --> 00:41:21.190
You have a guestbook
application.

00:41:21.190 --> 00:41:22.580
It has an IP address.

00:41:22.580 --> 00:41:24.090
It has a couple
of different ports

00:41:24.090 --> 00:41:25.800
that you can use to access it.

00:41:25.800 --> 00:41:30.510
And I can actually go ahead
and run a call command here

00:41:30.510 --> 00:41:32.447
against that application.

00:41:32.447 --> 00:41:34.530
And we'll go ahead and
change the IP address here.

00:41:38.650 --> 00:41:41.570
And notice that it returned
back some data telling me,

00:41:41.570 --> 00:41:46.000
hey, you're on
container 10 24 9.4.

00:41:46.000 --> 00:41:51.420
More importantly, I can switch
over here to my Windows node.

00:41:51.420 --> 00:41:52.780
That is the Windows node.

00:41:52.780 --> 00:41:55.270
That's the worker node
that's part of my cluster.

00:41:55.270 --> 00:41:57.180
And notice everything
is running on GCP here.

00:42:00.290 --> 00:42:02.460
I can switch on this node.

00:42:02.460 --> 00:42:07.730
And I can open up a web
browser and hit my guestbook

00:42:07.730 --> 00:42:09.350
application.

00:42:09.350 --> 00:42:10.850
And I can enter some data here.

00:42:10.850 --> 00:42:17.260
And I'm going to call
it audience1, 2, and 3.

00:42:17.260 --> 00:42:20.470
So if I go back
to my script here

00:42:20.470 --> 00:42:22.385
and I run the call
command again,

00:42:22.385 --> 00:42:24.010
I'm getting back the
data because I was

00:42:24.010 --> 00:42:26.417
able to query that application.

00:42:26.417 --> 00:42:28.000
So let's go ahead
and scale it and see

00:42:28.000 --> 00:42:29.680
some of the advantages
of Kubernetes.

00:42:29.680 --> 00:42:31.660
So I'm going to
run a command here

00:42:31.660 --> 00:42:33.580
that essentially is
instructing Kubernetes

00:42:33.580 --> 00:42:35.810
to increase the
deployment of guestbook

00:42:35.810 --> 00:42:37.720
to a three replica count.

00:42:37.720 --> 00:42:44.530
So if I run my
getpods command again,

00:42:44.530 --> 00:42:48.034
I get to see that guestbook now
has three instances running.

00:42:48.034 --> 00:42:49.450
Now, all three
instances are going

00:42:49.450 --> 00:42:51.280
to be load balanced
by Kubernetes.

00:42:51.280 --> 00:42:53.660
So as a developer, I don't
have to do anything extra.

00:42:53.660 --> 00:42:57.130
I can take advantage of the
complete backbone of Kubernetes

00:42:57.130 --> 00:43:00.590
that will handle routing, load
balancing, full tolerance,

00:43:00.590 --> 00:43:01.870
high availability.

00:43:01.870 --> 00:43:04.480
And all I had to do was
deploy my application.

00:43:04.480 --> 00:43:07.900
And all of those things
are taken for granted.

00:43:07.900 --> 00:43:09.920
And they're available
to me to use.

00:43:09.920 --> 00:43:14.230
So now I can go back to
my web browser again.

00:43:14.230 --> 00:43:18.210
And as I refresh, I can see the
endpoints changing sometimes.

00:43:18.210 --> 00:43:21.130
Now, there are sticky sessions
happening in the web browser,

00:43:21.130 --> 00:43:25.120
so you might not be able
to see this clearly.

00:43:25.120 --> 00:43:27.420
So I went ahead and
wrote the script here

00:43:27.420 --> 00:43:33.280
that will invoke the URI
for my application 20 times

00:43:33.280 --> 00:43:35.110
and print the contents.

00:43:35.110 --> 00:43:38.560
And notice here that
every time we invoke it,

00:43:38.560 --> 00:43:42.730
the IP address sometimes changes
because Kubernetes is basically

00:43:42.730 --> 00:43:46.720
load balancing the requests to
my application based on number

00:43:46.720 --> 00:43:48.160
of requests per second.

00:43:48.160 --> 00:43:52.862
So some of them are hitting
the container at p.4, 192,

00:43:52.862 --> 00:43:55.580
and I actually should have
a third one here as well.

00:43:55.580 --> 00:44:01.500
So if we go up, we should
be able to see the 187 IP

00:44:01.500 --> 00:44:02.700
address as well here.

00:44:02.700 --> 00:44:05.820
So those other three IP
addresses are part of my pod

00:44:05.820 --> 00:44:10.810
that are available for load
balancing for my application.

00:44:10.810 --> 00:44:12.570
And if we go back
to guestbook here,

00:44:12.570 --> 00:44:14.220
if I click the
environment variables,

00:44:14.220 --> 00:44:18.000
it is clearly evident here
that I'm running on Windows.

00:44:18.000 --> 00:44:21.550
This is Windows
Server containers.

00:44:21.550 --> 00:44:23.410
I'm able to see
all the information

00:44:23.410 --> 00:44:29.404
about the Redis cache that
is part of my application.

00:44:29.404 --> 00:44:32.870
And I can come
back into the node,

00:44:32.870 --> 00:44:35.960
and I can run Docker
commands, like Docker images,

00:44:35.960 --> 00:44:38.480
and see the images
that I was able to use

00:44:38.480 --> 00:44:40.340
for the deployment
of my application.

00:44:40.340 --> 00:44:43.760
Or run docker ps and look
at all the containers

00:44:43.760 --> 00:44:46.640
that were deployed by
Kubernetes as part of my app.

00:44:46.640 --> 00:44:48.500
So Kubernetes
handled everything,

00:44:48.500 --> 00:44:50.390
from the deployment
of containers,

00:44:50.390 --> 00:44:53.510
the setting up of the networking
backbone, the load balancing,

00:44:53.510 --> 00:44:56.360
as well as making sure that my
containers are up and running

00:44:56.360 --> 00:44:58.560
and highly available.

00:44:58.560 --> 00:45:00.258
Let's go ahead and
switch to the slides.

00:45:05.890 --> 00:45:09.340
So we showed you how
Kubernetes and the community

00:45:09.340 --> 00:45:11.550
is bringing Windows
Server support

00:45:11.550 --> 00:45:14.480
into the entire ecosystem.

00:45:14.480 --> 00:45:15.970
We're encouraging
everybody, if you

00:45:15.970 --> 00:45:18.700
